WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.743
Counter-deception shows up differently across security and risk workflows.

00:00:03.743 --> 00:00:08.194
In incident response, the team may see incomplete telemetry, noisy indicators,

00:00:08.194 --> 00:00:09.004
conflicting timelines,

00:00:09.004 --> 00:00:13.456
or signals that appear meaningful before the broader context is understood.

00:00:13.456 --> 00:00:19.931
A single artifact can pull the investigation toward one explanation while other evidence is still missing.

00:00:19.931 --> 00:00:24.383
Incident analysts should preserve the artifact, map the claim it supports,

00:00:24.383 --> 00:00:28.026
and test whether independent telemetry supports the same story.

00:00:28.026 --> 00:00:31.264
Fraud review adds identity, authorization, and intent questions.

00:00:31.264 --> 00:00:33.692
A transaction, account change, support interaction,

00:00:33.692 --> 00:00:38.549
or document may be real while the authority behind it is uncertain.

00:00:38.549 --> 00:00:43.001
Reviewers should distinguish account control from human identity, approval from legitimacy,

00:00:43.001 --> 00:00:46.643
and possession of information from authorization to use it.

00:00:46.643 --> 00:00:49.881
They should also be careful with people-centered conclusions,

00:00:49.881 --> 00:00:53.928
because poor collection or ambiguous context can harm legitimate users.

00:00:53.928 --> 00:00:55.952
Threat intelligence requires sourcing discipline.

00:00:55.952 --> 00:01:00.404
Claims about actors, tools, infrastructure, campaigns, or targeting can be useful,

00:01:00.404 --> 00:01:03.642
but they vary widely in provenance and confidence.

00:01:03.642 --> 00:01:08.094
Analysts should look for original sourcing, collection context, date ranges, definitions,

00:01:08.094 --> 00:01:10.117
and corroboration with internal telemetry.

00:01:10.117 --> 00:01:14.974
An indicator may be valid in one context and irrelevant in another.

00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:19.021
Attribution language should be especially cautious when evidence is indirect.

00:01:19.021 --> 00:01:21.045
Not every inconsistency proves deception.

00:01:21.045 --> 00:01:23.878
Logging gaps, translation issues, redaction, ingestion delay,

00:01:23.878 --> 00:01:27.520
and ordinary process failures can all create strange-looking evidence.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.354
Counter-deception is not a license to overclaim.

00:01:30.354 --> 00:01:34.401
It is a method for deciding what confidence is justified.

00:01:34.401 --> 00:01:38.043
Clear language such as confirmed, assessed, plausible, uncorroborated, inconsistent,

00:01:38.043 --> 00:01:42.900
or unknown helps leaders understand the difference between evidence, judgment, and uncertainty.
